<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 10: Accounting for Variability                              -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-accounting-variability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Accounting for Variability</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-ch10-variability">
    <p>
      We looked at observing and measuring variability in
      <xref ref="ch-variability"/>; for example, how spread out values are
      around a centre, and how to put a number on that spread.
      We saw that life expectancy across African countries had a standard
      deviation of around 9.6 years in 2007, that Rwanda's collapse in 1992
      produced a squared distance from the mean so large it dominated the
      variance for that year, and that the IQR is more robust to such extremes
      than the standard deviation.
      Those are all answers to the question: <em>how much</em> does this vary?
    </p>

    <p>
      This chapter looks at <em>why</em> it varies.
      Or more precisely, what portion of the variation can be accounted for, and
      what portion remains unaccounted for.
      This is the distinction between the three types of variability introduced
      in <xref ref="sec-accounting-journeys"/>: accounted-for, unaccounted-for,
      and natural.
      Measuring tells you the size of the spread, while accounting for it tells
      you where it comes from.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-accounting-journeys">
    <title>The Same Journey, Every Day Different</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In August 2026, on a Friday evening, I was leaving Nairobi's CBD to
        catch a late flight home to London.
        I had a flight at 11:40pm and ordered an Uber around 7:30pm.
        The first driver appeared, then cancelled while I was outside looking
        for them — I had lost wifi in the chaos of the streets and could not
        track them through the app.
        The same thing happened with a second driver.
        When I finally got wifi, the second driver agreed to pick me up from a
        hotel across the road, but had to turn around first because of the
        one-way street.
        I waited twenty minutes on that pavement, surrounded by cars, and could
        not find them anywhere.
        It was completely gridlocked, and he was still turning around.
      </p>

      <p>
        I eventually walked through the traffic hoping to find wifi and order a
        new driver somewhere away from the worst of the gridlock.
        A worker outside a café noticed me standing on the pavement, clearly
        lost and increasingly frantic, and insisted I come in.
        She sat me down, connected me to the wifi, and gave me a moment to
        think.
        Without that kindness I am not sure what I would have done.
        I caught the flight.
      </p>

      <p>
        The app said the third taxi was ten minutes away.
        It took forty-five.
        The app had estimated ten minutes — and I had believed it, because I had
        no reason not to.
        I sat watching the little car barely move, block after block, as the
        minutes ticked by and my flight window got smaller.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ask someone from Nairobi what happened, and they will tell you
        immediately: it was Friday evening, in the CBD, at 7:30pm.
        That is a journey you plan hours around, or you do not make it.
        The traffic leaving the central business district on a Friday evening is
        not unpredictable.
        It is entirely predictable.
        It is just that I did not know to predict it.
      </p>

      <p>
        In the previous chapter, we measured variability — how spread out values
        are, and how to summarise that spread in a single number.
        But measuring variability and understanding it are two different things.
        My taxi journey was not just variable.
        It was variable for reasons — some of which I should have known, some of
        which I could not have known, and some of which were simply random.
        This chapter is about separating those three things.
      </p>

      <p>
        Imagine you had no information at all about how long a boda-boda ride
        across Nairobi would take.
        No knowledge of rush hour, no sense of which roads are usually busy, no
        awareness that Fridays are worse than Tuesdays.
        Every day, you would treat each journey as if it were identical to every
        other — and you would be wrong, repeatedly, in ways that cost you time,
        money, and patience.
        You would book the same amount of time for a 7am ride and a 5pm ride.
        You would be surprised, every single Friday afternoon, by the traffic.
        You would have no way to plan.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now imagine you know one thing: rush hour is slower.
        Suddenly you can plan.
        You leave earlier on the days that matter, and book less time for the
        quiet morning run.
        You may still be wrong sometimes — you cannot know about the accident on
        Ngong Road, or the blocked junction in town — but you are systematically
        less wrong than before.
        Knowing even one source of variation and accounting for it makes your
        predictions better.
        My mistake on that Friday evening was not unavoidable.
        It was a failure to account for something that was entirely knowable.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is what statisticians mean by the three types of variability.
        Some variation is <term>accounted-for variability</term>: you know what
        causes it, you have measured it, and you can use it to make better
        predictions.
        Time of day is exactly this — once you know it is 5pm on a Friday in
        Nairobi's CBD, you already know something useful about the journey
        ahead.
        Some variation is <term>unaccounted-for variability</term>: other things
        affect the journey — accidents, roadworks, the school term starting —
        but you have not measured them and cannot include them in your
        predictions.
        They are real causes of variation, making your predictions less reliable
        even when you have accounted for everything else you can.
        And some variation is just <term>natural variability</term>: the
        boda-boda rider caught a green light, or took a slightly different line
        through the roundabout.
        No amount of information would have predicted it.
        It simply happened.
      </p>

      <p>
        The problem is not the natural variability — any honest prediction has
        to live with it.
        The problem is mistaking unaccounted-for variation for natural
        variation.
        If you assume that everything you cannot account for is just noise, you
        will underestimate how wrong your predictions can be, and you will miss
        the fact that some of that wrongness has a cause that could, in
        principle, be found and understood.
        A boda-boda rider who thinks the variation in their journey times is
        just random bad luck will never think to check whether there is a
        pattern — whether certain roads are systematically worse, whether the
        school term changes things, whether the weather is a factor.
        Those patterns are real.
        Mistaking them for noise means never looking for them.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-accounting-traffic">
      <title>Accounting for Traffic Variation in Nairobi</title>

      <p>
        The visualisations by TomTom displayed in Ch9 make the three types of
        variability visible in a way that numbers alone cannot.
      </p>

      <p>
        Recall <xref ref="fig-tomtom-heatmap-week"/> which shows travel time
        across every hour of every day of the week, averaged across 2025.
        Each cell represents one two-hour slot on one day of the week; darker
        red means longer journey times, lighter pink means shorter.
        The pattern is immediately visible: weekday mornings and evenings are
        dark, weekend days are lighter, and the middle of the night is the
        lightest of all.
        This is <term>accounted-for variability</term> made visible: the
        variation that follows a predictable pattern, week after week, and that
        anyone who knows Nairobi already carries in their head.
        If I had looked at this chart before ordering my Uber on that Friday
        evening in August, I would have seen immediately that 7:30pm on a Friday
        is one of the darker cells on the whole grid.
      </p>

      <p>
        <xref ref="fig-tomtom-map-morning"/> and
        <xref ref="fig-tomtom-map-evening"/> show where in Nairobi that
        congestion falls during the morning and evening rush hours — not just
        when, but where.
        Yellow hexagons indicate moderate congestion; orange and red indicate
        heavier delays.
        The CBD and the main arterial roads radiating outward from it are
        visibly darker than the outer suburbs.
        During the morning rush, the congestion concentrates in the city centre
        as commuters flow in; during the evening rush, it spreads more broadly
        as people try to get home.
        This spatial pattern is also displaying
        <term>accounted-for variability</term> — predictable, measurable, and
        useful for planning.
      </p>

      <p>
        But even knowing the time of day and the location is not enough to
        predict any specific journey perfectly.
        The fourth image illustrates why.
        This is a screenshot I have just taken showing live congestion in
        Nairobi right now <fn>25th August 2026, 18:48 EAT</fn>, against the
        usual congestion level for the same time slot.
        The orange line is what is happening right now, the dotted yellow line
        is what typically happens at this time.
        On Monday morning, live congestion spiked to nearly 90 percent, well
        above the usual level of around 65 percent at that hour.
        On Tuesday afternoon, at the time this image was captured, live
        congestion was running at over 100 percent, higher than the typical
        level, and still climbing.
        Something was making that particular day worse than a typical Tuesday.
        Perhaps an accident, or roadworks, or nothing identifiable at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is <term>unaccounted-for variability</term> in action.
        TomTom's usual congestion line represents what the accounted-for
        variability predicts, the pattern that repeats week after week.
        The gap between that line and the live reading is the part that the
        pattern cannot account for.
        Some of that gap has a cause, something happened on that road on that
        day that pushed congestion above its normal level.
        And some of it is just <term>natural variability</term>: small
        fluctuations that remain even after everything predictable has been
        accounted for.
        The chart shows all three types of variability at once: the repeating
        pattern (accounted for), the unaccounted for spike above it (unaccounted
        for), and the jagged noise around both lines (natural).
      </p>

      <p>
        <xref ref="fig-tomtom-live-congestion"/> displays a live congestion
        chart from TomTom.
        This makes all three types of variability visible in a single image.
        The dotted yellow line shows the usual congestion level for each time
        slot — the pattern that repeats week after week, the part that is
        accounted for.
        The orange line shows what actually happened on a particular Monday and
        Tuesday.
        The gap between the two lines is the part the usual pattern could not
        predict: something happened on those specific days that pushed
        congestion above its normal level.
        An accident, perhaps.
        A road closure.
        Something real, but unaccounted for.
        And the jagged noise around both lines — the small moment-to-moment
        fluctuations — is natural variability: irreducible randomness that would
        remain even if we knew everything measurable.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-live-congestion">
        <caption>
          Live versus usual congestion level in Nairobi across Monday and
          Tuesday, with live speed shown below.
          The orange line shows actual congestion; the dotted yellow line shows
          the typical level for that time slot.
          The gap between the two lines represents variation that the usual
          pattern does not predict — unaccounted-for and natural variability.
          Source: TomTom Traffic Index, captured August 2026.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_live_congestion.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A line chart showing congestion level as a percentage on the
              y-axis and time across Monday and Tuesday on the x-axis.
              An orange solid line shows live congestion and a dotted yellow
              line shows the usual congestion level.
              Both lines show peaks around 8am and 5pm on each day.
              On Monday afternoon, the orange live line spikes to nearly 90
              percent, substantially above the yellow usual line of around 65
              percent.
              On Tuesday afternoon, the live line reaches over 100 percent and
              continues rising at the right edge of the chart, labelled NOW,
              again running above the usual level.
              Below the congestion chart, a bar chart shows live speed in km/h,
              with speeds dropping to around 10 km/h at peak congestion periods.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Similar patterns have been documented in Kampala, Uganda, where analysis
        of close to a million boda-boda trips found significant variation in
        travel speed across different periods of the day.
        The accounted-for variation — the time-of-day pattern — was strong and
        consistent.
        But within any given time slot, individual trips still varied,
        reflecting the unaccounted-for and natural variability that no amount of
        time-of-day information could eliminate (Saddier, 2025).
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
        Nairobi city profile.
        Available at:
        <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/" visual="tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi"/>.
        Saddier, S.
        (2025).
        <q>Are motorcycle taxis competing with 
      collective public transport?</q>
        <em>Research in Transportation Economics</em>, 111, 101562.
        <url href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.retrec.2025.101562"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch9-variability-illustration">
    <title>Illustration of Variability</title>

    <p>
      Before measuring variability, it helps to see what the different types
      actually look like.
      We can build them up from scratch, starting with a dataset that has no
      variability at all.
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>No variability</title>

      <p>
        If <m>y</m> is always the same value regardless of anything else, there
        is nothing to explain and nothing to measure.
        Every point sits on a flat horizontal line.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <program language="r">
      <code>
      x &lt;- 1:10
      y &lt;- rep(1, 10)
      df0 &lt;- data.frame(x, y)

      ggplot(df0, aes(x = x, y = y)) +
        geom_point() +
        geom_line() +
        labs(title = "No variability: y is always 1",
             y = "y", x = "x") +
        theme_bw()
      </code>
    </program>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-no-variability">
      <caption>
        No variability.
        <m>y = 1</m> regardless of <m>x</m>.
        There is nothing to account for, and nothing to measure.
      </caption>

      <image source="images/ch9_no_variability.png" width="70%">
        <description>
          <p>
            A scatter plot with x on the horizontal axis and y on the vertical
            axis.
            All ten points sit at exactly y = 1, connected by a flat horizontal
            line.
            There is no spread in the y values at all.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Accounted-for variability only</title>

      <p>
        Now let <m>y = x</m>.
        There is variability in <m>y</m> — the values change — but all of it is
        accounted for by <m>x</m>.
        As <m>x</m> increases, <m>y</m> increases by exactly the same amount.
        No randomness, no surprises.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <program language="r">
      <code>
      x &lt;- 1:10
      y &lt;- x
      df1 &lt;- data.frame(x, y)

      ggplot(df1, aes(x = x, y = y)) +
        geom_point() +
        geom_line() +
        labs(title = "Accounted for variability only: y = x",
             y = "y", x = "x") +
        theme_bw()
      </code>
    </program>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-accounted-for-only">
      <caption>
        Accounted for variability only.
        <m>y = x</m>: all variability in <m>y</m> is accounted for by <m>x</m>.
      </caption>

      <image source="images/ch9_accounted for_only.png" width="70%">
        <description>
          <p>
            A scatter plot where all ten points fall exactly on a straight
            diagonal line from the lower left to the upper right.
            There is no scatter around the line.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Accounted for and natural variability</title>

      <p>
        Now add some random noise.
        The relationship between <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> is still there.
        <m>y</m> still tends to increase with <m>x</m>, but the points no longer
        fall exactly on the line.
        Some additional randomness is pushing them above or below where the line
        would predict.
        This is natural variability: noise that remains even after <m>x</m> is
        accounted for.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <program language="r">
      <code>
      x &lt;- 1:10
      y &lt;- x + rnorm(10)
      df2 &lt;- data.frame(x, y)

      ggplot(df2, aes(x = x, y = y)) +
        geom_point() +
        geom_line(data = df1, aes(x = x, y = y),
                  linetype = "dashed", colour = "grey50") +
        labs(title = "Accounted for + natural variability: y = x + error",
             subtitle = "Dashed line shows y = x",
             y = "y", x = "x") +
        theme_bw()
      </code>
    </program>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-accounted-for-natural">
      <caption>
        accounted for and natural variability.
        <m>y = x + \varepsilon</m>: points scatter around the line <m>y = x</m>
        (shown dashed) due to random noise.
        The trend is still visible but no longer perfect.
      </caption>

      <image source="images/ch9_accounted for_natural.png" width="70%">
        <description>
          <p>
            A scatter plot where the ten points are scattered around a dashed
            diagonal line representing y = x.
            Some points are above the line and some below, with no obvious
            pattern to the deviations.
            The overall upward trend is still visible.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Accounted for, unaccounted-for, and natural variability</title>

      <p>
        Finally, introduce a second variable <m>z</m> that also influences
        <m>y</m>, but which we do not have in our dataset.
        In the code below, <m>z</m> takes the value 1 for the first five
        observations and 10 for the last five.
        We can see the effect it has: the points no longer scatter evenly around
        the line, but jump up noticeably in the second half.
        But if we did not know <m>z</m> existed, we would have no way to explain
        that jump.
        It would simply look like unusually large random variation.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <program language="r">
      <code>
      x  &lt;- 1:10
      z  &lt;- c(rep(1, 5), rep(10, 5))  # hidden variable
      y  &lt;- x + z + rnorm(10)
      df3 &lt;- data.frame(x, y)

      ggplot(df3, aes(x = x, y = y)) +
        geom_point() +
        geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE,
                    colour = "#44575E", linewidth = 0.8) +
        labs(title = "Accounted for, unaccounted-for, and natural variability",
             subtitle = "The line fits poorly because z is not in the data",
             y = "y", x = "x") +
        theme_bw()
      </code>
    </program>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-all-variability">
      <caption>
        Accounted for, unaccounted for, and natural variability.
        <m>y = x + z + \varepsilon</m>, but <m>z</m> is not in the data.
        The line of best fit through <m>x</m> alone fits poorly — the points in
        the second half jump above the line because of <m>z</m>, which we cannot
        see.
      </caption>

      <image source="images/ch9_all_variability.png" width="70%">
        <description>
          <p>
            A scatter plot where the first five points scatter around the lower
            part of a fitted line and the last five points scatter around a
            noticeably higher position.
            The fitted line is a poor summary of the data because it cannot
            account for the jump caused by the hidden variable z.
            The residuals are large and systematically patterned rather than
            randomly scattered.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      This last scenario is the most realistic.
      In the real world, we never have all the variables.
      There will always be something we have not measured that is influencing
      the outcome.
      The question is how much of the variability we can account for with what
      we do have — and how honest we are about what remains unaccounted for.
      The Nairobi traffic data is exactly this: time of day accounts for some of
      the variability in journey time, but unknown events (accidents, roadworks,
      broken-down matatus) account for more, and pure randomness accounts for
      the rest.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch10-stack">
    <title>Variability STACK Question</title>

